WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.877
Sometimes we want the first available value from several possible columns.

00:00:04.877 --> 00:00:09.832
The coalesce function returns the first value that is not null.

00:00:09.832 --> 00:00:14.627
In this example, we first try a cleaned email address.

00:00:14.627 --> 00:00:19.287
If no usable email is available, we try the phone number.

00:00:19.287 --> 00:00:24.299
If neither is available, we return the phrase no contact method.

00:00:24.857 --> 00:00:28.594
Think of this as a fallback strategy, not a correction.

00:00:28.594 --> 00:00:36.104
If a customer's email is missing and we use the phone number as the preferred contact, we have not repaired the email.

00:00:36.104 --> 00:00:39.874
We have only selected another available contact method.

00:00:39.874 --> 00:00:48.944
That distinction matters because a fallback can be useful for a report or workflow while the original data quality

00:00:48.944 --> 00:00:55.024
problem still exists. When you run the query, identify which records use email, which fall back to phone,

00:00:55.024 --> 00:00:57.692
and which have no available contact method.
